WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.232
Start cloud inference with one model and one measured request path.

00:00:05.232 --> 00:00:14.231
Download model artifacts from an approved source, verify the expected version, and place them in storage appropriate to their reuse.

00:00:14.231 --> 00:00:21.974
Quantization can reduce VRAM demand and make a less expensive instance viable, but it may affect quality.

00:00:21.974 --> 00:00:28.042
Benchmark candidate quantizations with prompts and context lengths that represent the intended application.

00:00:28.042 --> 00:00:32.437
A serving API separates clients from the model process.

00:00:32.437 --> 00:00:40.180
Configure authentication, request limits, timeouts, health checks, and structured errors before treating it as a shared service.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:45.412
Concurrency increases the number of simultaneous sequences and their memory demand.

00:00:45.412 --> 00:00:53.155
Batching may improve accelerator utilization and total throughput but can increase the latency of an individual request.

00:00:53.155 --> 00:01:00.480
Measure time to first token, prompt processing, generation rate, queue time, and completed requests over time.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.363
Network distance matters.

00:01:02.363 --> 00:01:11.780
A fast model in a distant region may feel slower than a modest model placed near its users and application data.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:16.175
Include gateway, queue, and transfer delays in end-to-end tests.

00:01:16.175 --> 00:01:23.918
Exercise long prompts, multiple clients, failures, and restart behavior rather than relying on a single short request.

00:01:23.918 --> 00:01:35.010
Autoscaling can add or remove instances according to demand, but GPU startup may be slow because capacity must be allocated, software initialized, and models loaded.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:40.241
Define minimum capacity, scale triggers, cooldown periods, and maximum cost exposure.

00:01:40.241 --> 00:01:45.473
Autoscaling is not a substitute for capacity planning or admission control.

00:01:45.473 --> 00:01:53.635
Establish a stable single-instance baseline first, then scale when real workload measurements show where additional resources improve service.
